            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your 1971 house has a few areas of air leakage heat loss that should be addressed:



** The ceiling area above the upper front main entrance door



** The wall section in the living area adjacent to that entrance door was very cold with air coming in around wall plugs &amp; switches



** The cathedral ceilings - all along both sides of the main support beams



** The bathroom exhaust fans in both upper floor bathrooms



** The lower level wall that adjoins the garage - many holes &amp; uninsulated



Savings: I modeled a modest 10% reduction in overall air leakage, that could potentially save $ 179 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.0 GJ, and qualify for $ 500 in rebates. For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate. Achieving 20% qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house include the lower and upper levels at the back of the house plus the upper floor living space above the garage. The lower level is made up of various wall sections that include some rigid insulation along the sides &amp; rear plus a framed/insulated section along the front. There is also a section of wall that adjoins the garage that has no insulation. The upper floors are all 2x6 construction with R20 batt insulation.



At a minimum, the wall between the lower level &amp; the garage should be properly insulated and air/vapour/gas sealed to reduce heat loss from the house, air/moisture transfer &amp; especially combustion products from entering the house. I have modeled adding a minimum R10 to all of the walls of the house, with R30 for the uninsulated wall between the house &amp; garage.



NOTE: You are currently heating the garage, as this is helping to keep the rooms above warmer (and reduces heat loss through that uninsulated wall that adjoins the lower level of the house behind). The heat ductwork (for those rooms above) passes through the garage and is uninsulated - and this would be a problem if the garage was unheated !! Heating the garage does represent significant energy use and something that is not modeled as part of the EnerGuide Home evaluation.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). Most homeowners participating in this program are already committed to (or seriously considering) an exterior siding + new windows retrofit ............ so our rebate is meant to help cover some of the cost of the additional insulation to go behind that new siding.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R10, you could potentially save $ 726 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 28.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



NOTE: Without an eligible exterior wall insulation upgrade, many of the other rebates in this report are not eligible. This is further outlined in the "Additional Comments" section of this report.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home (lower &amp; upper levels) must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.</mainWalls>
                <ceilingsCathedral>

Your house has all cathedral ceilings. We discussed that, from the ceiling repairs you have recently made, there appears to be about 7" - 8" of insulation in the ceiling - representing R28. The walls of the house have 5.5" (R20), so I would expect to see a minimum R28 in the ceiling.



You had mentioned that you wanted to add some interior rigid insulation, however were not sure how much you really wanted to add as you want to maintain a decent profile for the exposed beams. As the current insulation is &gt;R24, there are no available rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program &amp; you need to increase to a minimum R40 if you want to access a $200 rebate from Greener Homes.



Rather than trying to chase a small rebate, I just modeled the anticipated energy savings associated with adding a basic R7.5 (1.5" of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation) installed on the interior surface. 



Savings:

By adding R7.5 to 100% of the ceiling, you could potentially save $ 210 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.3 GJ. I suspect one of the associated benefits of completing this upgrade is the reduction in air leakage heat loss by properly sealing all of the perimeter seams of the ceiling - especially where it adjoins the beams. See my section on air sealing for those savings.</ceilingsCathedral>
                <exposedFloors>

There is a large section of exposed floor for the heated living space above the garage. There was no access to see how much insulation is currently in there, however it would be common for that age of housing to have R20 in that floor (especially when there is R20 in the exterior walls &amp; R28 in the cathedral ceiling).



NOTE: You are currently heating the garage, as this is helping to keep the rooms above warmer (and reduces heat loss through that uninsulated wall that adjoins the lower level of the house behind). The heat ductwork (for those rooms above) passes through the garage and is uninsulated - and this would be a problem if the garage was unheated !! Heating the garage does represent significant energy use and something that is not modeled as part of the EnerGuide Home evaluation.



With your new boiler and the ability to have both hydronic heat or forced air via the heat exchanger, you have the opportunity to eliminate that ductwork that feeds the upper floor &amp; convert the upper bedrooms and living area to in-floor hydronic heat. This would involve a retrofit from below (ie: from inside the garage).



There are a couple of grants you could qualify for here:

Adding a minimum R20 to 100% of the exposed floor could qualify you for:

- $250 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (doubled to $500 if you are also participating in the DHERP program by adding a minimum R10 to the exterior walls of your house)

- $455 from the Greener Homes Program



If you increase the insulation value to a minimum R50 (with a minimum R10 continuous below the joists), you could qualify for:

- $250 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (doubled to $500 if you are also participating in the DHERP program by adding a minimum R10 to the exterior walls of your house)

- $1,500 from the Greener Homes Program



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exposed floor up to R50 (with a minimum R10 continuous below the joists) you could potentially save $ 99 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,750 in rebates (combined Energy Efficiency Rebate Program and the Greener Homes Program). In fact, the savings should be even greater, as you will not need to keep the temperature in your garage as high !!!



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $455 is available for adding insulation to exposed floors. Insulating exposed floors may result in energy savings and improve comfort in rooms above exposed floors. To be eligible to receive a grant for exposed floors, you must insulate the entire exposed floor area above the garage with a minimum of RSI 3.52 (R-20) insulation for $455. See my comments above for an opportunity to increase this rebate to $1,500.</exposedFloors>
                <foundation>

The lower level floor is essentially bedrock which is equivalent to an uninsulated concrete slab. In newer construction, the floor is designed to have the slab (or bedrock) insulated. Although this is a tough thing for you to accomplish as a retrofit, I thought I would model the energy savings if the floor had R12 (eg. 3" of expanded polystyrene rigid foam board).



Savings:

By adding R12 to 100% of the lower level slab floor, you could potentially save $ 409 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 16.1 GJ. There are currently no rebates for this upgrade.</foundation>
                <windows>

Many of the windows in your house have been upgraded, however there are 7 that may still require upgrading (the 3 small windows in the lower level @ the rear, the 1 large picture window on the upper level @ the rear, the 2 glass block windows &amp; perhaps the one window on the lower level @ the front). I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading these 7 windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing all of the above windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 255 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 10.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,750 in rebates (7 windows max @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $125 or $250 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem />
                <Renewables>

Renewable Energy Systems:



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home:



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Savings: Just in case you may be interested in something fun, I modeled a 5 kW PV system, flush mounted on the rear facing roof (East), directly connected to the grid, without a battery-based standby power system. This is estimated to produce about 3,891 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,284, and qualify for the full $5,000 from the Canada Greener Homes program.

</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has three toilets and the one in the lower level is an older model that uses a lot of water &amp; should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 1000 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total above foundation, exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits, and excludes any new additions. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the Arctic Energy Alliance.



DHERP rebates are provided on a first-come, first-serve basis for as long as funding remains available. Receiving a pre-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation does not in itself guarantee the availability of funding. Homeowners who have demonstrated their intention to proceed with an eligible above foundation, exterior wall insulation upgrade will be:

      A. Provided with a conditional "Pre-Authorization" of DHERP funding, if funding is still available; or

      B. Placed on a DHERP funding waiting list, should funding become available



For more information and program guidelines, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



As you research if you will indeed participate in the DHERP, please note the upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $6,750 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $3,000 for the wall insulation

     - $   500 for the exposed floor insulation (above the garage)

     - $1,750 for upgrading 7 windows with eligible ESTAR units

     - $   500 for a minimum 10% air leakage reduction

     - $1,000 Bonus if you achieve a minimum 20% total energy reduction 



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible wall insulation upgrade, than you are not eligible for the DHERP - and none of the rebates outlined above will apply.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



????????? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

????????? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

????????? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

????????? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

????????? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

????????? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (for your house, the range hood, 2 bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 2.7 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current boiler is high efficiency &amp; directly vented &amp; not susceptible to back-drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood

        - 2 older bathroom fans

        - The clothes dryer



As both bathroom fans are older, and allow a fair bit of air leakage, they should be replaced with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified units. For the new bathroom fans, you may want to consider installing a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following Nov. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.40 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $0.91 per liter 

Propane - $0.61 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

